I reproduced both holes against the current code, using a scratch --store so the repo's record store was not touched. (1) An empty --transcripts dir with --record printed transcriptsScanned 0 and stored a bench:trace-bench-lite record with final_status accept and no F0. (2) A leaking transcript with no meta (label '') maps to arm 'unknown'. markContaminated never matches it, so contaminatedRuns stays 0 and no F0 is raised. In the held-verdict variant the stored record was accept with failed_gates [], which clears the F0/F4 conditions of the L3 checklist gate. Existing tests cover only the missing-flag case and a well-formed verifier label.

I also found a third hole that strengthens the finding. The panel label regex at lib.mjs:934 uses `[a-z-]+`, so a correctly labelled leak from the production 'qa-e2e' critic is also unattributable. The fix should widen that class to `[a-z0-9-]+`, in addition to:
- requiring transcripts.length > 0, ideally at least the number of runs, for --record;
- treating any 'unknown' key as an F0 (fail-closed) flag;
- adding tests for the empty-dir, unknown-label and qa-e2e-label cases.

Severity 'important' is justified because the L3 trust gate relies on this record.

I tried to refute this and could not; the claim holds as stated. In scripts/trace/cli.mjs, git() (lines 91-97) catches every execFileSync error and returns null. It sets no maxBuffer, so Node's 1 MB default applies. denylist-check (lines 168-181) maps null to an empty list and returns ok:true with exit 0.

I reproduced the failure three ways, each with denylisted files present:
- a directory that is not a git repo: ok:true, checked:0, exit 0;
- a repo with an unborn HEAD and the denylisted file staged: also exit 0;
- a real ENOBUFS case, with 3.1 MB of untracked-path output: exit 0. Excluding node_modules on the same tree flipped the result to ok:false, checked:21, exit 1.

On a clean tree the real repo prints the same ok:true, checked:0 output, so a consumer cannot tell clean from failed. loop.md:108-111 acts only on a hit, so the failure reads as a pass. This contradicts the documented fail-closed principle in trace.md:71, the writer at cli.mjs:190-193 and the sibling gate at loop-iteration.js:219. No test covers this path.

The recommended fix is sound and bounded:
- use a throwing git variant with a large maxBuffer;
- exit 2 'could not verify' when any git call fails;
- add a test in a temporary directory that is not a repo, plus one that forces ENOBUFS or an unborn HEAD.

The extra 'checked=0 after a patch was applied' heuristic is optional: a patch with no net effect could legitimately produce checked:0. Severity 'important' is justified, since this is the backstop for the safety denylist at L2/L3.
